    "md5": "e856d40e43479b05c702674a42942535",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1765,
    "chunk_text": "Authorities examine crypto regulations as digital economy drive gathers pace\n\nSri Lanka is moving closer to establishing a regulatory framework for virtual assets, as the policymakers and financial regulators grapple with the growing risks posed by the unregulated cryptocurrency trading, offshore platforms and cross-border digital transactions.\n\nThe issue took centre stage yesterday as the Digital Economy Ministry and Securities and Exchange Commission of Sri Lanka (SEC) brought together the regulators, policymakers and industry experts to discuss the contours of a regulatory roadmap for virtual assets, a market that continues to operate largely outside the country\u2019s formal financial regulatory framework.\n\nThe governments across the world accelerate efforts to regulate digital assets amid the rising retail participation, increasing institutional interest and concerns over money laundering, fraud and investor protection.\n\nThe experts addressing the forum warned that a significant portion of Sri Lankan virtual asset activity currently takes place through peer-to-peer channels and offshore platforms, creating challenges for the regulators seeking to monitor the transactions and safeguard investors.\n\nThe Digital Economy Ministry noted that the absence of a regulatory framework leaves the participants exposed to operational, financial and compliance risks while limiting the country\u2019s ability to benefit from the emerging opportunities in the digital asset ecosystem.\n\nThe awareness session, jointly organised by the Digital Economy Ministry and SEC, under the theme \u2018Let\u2019s Together for a Safer Digital Future\u2019, focused on the lessons that could be drawn from the jurisdictions that have already established frameworks for virtual assets."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
